> It is easy to make the argument that[ smart phones] are no longer a[ luxury]. They represent a world of[ music] and[ messages] that fit perfectly into the back pocket of a pair of[ jeans].
Our phones deliver[ reminders] of[ err]ands, maintain our[ calendar], provide driving[ directions], and of course[ Siri] is on call 24/7 for[ everything] else. So if a[ device] is so essential, is it possible for it to take control? M[mmmm]…..
Do you spin into a momentary[ panic] when you lose sight of it?
Do you[ imagine] that you hear it ringing when it isn�[�]t?
Is checking your[ phone] one of the first few[ things] you do in the[ morning] and one of the last[ things] you do at[ night]?
Do you[ text] while walking because you need to respond immediately?
Do you have[ multiple phone charg]ers: in the[ car], office, home,[ diaper] bag?
If you[ forget] it at home, do you turn around and risk being late for an[ appointment] to[ retrieve] it?
Do you review[ emails] during[ conversations]?
Do you use the[ phone] as an[ alarm clock] because it is sitting on your[ night]stand anyway?
If you[ haven’]t received an[ alert] in a while, do you check to make sure your[ phone] is still working?
Have you[ bought] several cases and[ accessories] for your[ phone] to treat it to something[ nice]?
If you[ answered yes] to any of these, you might have[ issues]. Text me and we[’]ll[ talk] about it.

