=== Text #7 ===
> Burn serious[ calories] while winning cash in your NCAA office pool with the Men’s Health March Madness Bracket Challenge Workout!
Got your March Madness[ bracket] filled out and a[ little] cash on the line? Why not put up a[ little] pride and muscle, too?
Team your NCAA[ basketball] tournament[ bracket] with a[ workout] challenge to[ motivate] you to do extra exercise, suggests Jim Cotta, CSCS, a former strength coach of the LA[ Lakers] and author of Men’s Health Workout War.
Here’s how it[ works]: Invite your friends, family or co-workers to join the challenge. Fill out your March Madness brackets.
Based on whether your chosen teams win or lose, you do the corresponding number of[ reps] of a certain exercise.
Win[ners] do fewer[ reps]; Losers do more.[ Everybody] gets in shape.
You[’]ll perform an[ exercise] exercise for each game that is played that day throughout the tournament. So that[’]s a[ circuit] of 16[ exercises] after both days 1 and 2, which are Round 1 games.
In round 2, you will[ circuit] through the 8[ exercises], and then repeat. So you’ll do 8[ exercises], 2[ times] each.
Round 3: Do 4[ exercises], 3[ times] each.
Round 4: Do 2[ exercises] 4[ times] each.
Round 5: Do 2[ exercises], 5[ times] each.
Round 6 (Ch[ampions]hip Game): 1 exercise, 6[ times].
“If you don[’]t have a team in the[ championship] game,[ sorry dude], it�[�]s 6 sets of 20[ Bur]pees,” says Cotta.
For any game in which you have neither seed playing, you do 20[ Bur]pees.
Complete your[ workout] the day after the games have been played, so you will have[ workouts] to do on March 20, 21, 22, 23, 27, 28, 29, 30 and April 5 and 7.
